  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2t,3{t}^{2}-12t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ \u27e8\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=0, \\\\ 3t(t-4)=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary.",
